+### ADR-008: External agent workflow tools remain optional and evidence-gated (2026-04-25)
+
+**Context:** The Dicklesworthstone agent-workflow repositories contain useful primitives: session-history search (`cass`), warning-only bug scanning (`ubs`), destructive-command explanation packs (`dcg`), local task graphs (`br`/`bv`), and agent settings backup (`asb`). They also bring installer, version, platform, and workflow gravity that does not fit SaneProcess as a default dependency.
+
+**Decision:** SaneProcess evaluates these tools through `ruby scripts/SaneMaster.rb external_tools`. The command uses safe read-only probes when tools are already installed and reports concrete adopt/watch/skip recommendations. It does not install them, does not add them to default verify, and does not replace canonical SaneProcess paths such as `status`, `check_inbox`, `verify`, `gate_review`, or Mini-first runtime proof. The command is strict Mini-first by default because local fallback would evaluate the wrong host; `--local` is reserved for explicit controller-machine inspection.
+
+**Consequences:**
+- `cass` can become an optional research surface if it proves useful on real session history.
+- `ubs` can only graduate as warning-only evidence after false positives and runtime cost are measured.
+- Initial Mini evidence on 2026-04-25 kept both installed candidates out of default adoption: `cass` had useful session metadata but an unhealthy search index, and `ubs` failed on a module checksum mismatch before scanning.
+
+### ADR-009: Privacy-safe analytics distinguish aggregate business signals from spyware (2026-04-25)
+
+**Context:** SaneApps needs conversion, download, launch, update, purchase, activation, crash, and broad feature-adoption metrics to run the business. Overbroad `no analytics` and `no telemetry` claims make normal aggregate measurement look like a violation, while the real customer concern is spyware: hidden personal/content/device surveillance or selling user-level data.
+
+**Decision:** SaneApps allows plainly disclosed, privacy-preserving aggregate analytics. The standard forbids personal or invasive analytics payloads: user IDs, cookies used to identify people, ad IDs, cross-site/app tracking, raw IP retention for analytics, customer content, clipboard data, file names, app-internal clicked URLs, browser history, host entries, videos, sales/order data, API keys, names, emails, license keys, and user-level sale/sharing. New privacy copy must avoid absolute `no analytics`, `no telemetry`, `zero tracking`, or `no network requests` claims unless they are literally true for that app.
+
+**Consequences:**
+- App and website instrumentation should use allowlisted event names and fields.
+- Product privacy pages should disclose aggregate analytics in customer language instead of hiding it or overstating it.
+- Website analytics such as Cloudflare can remain only when the site discloses cookie-free aggregate website analytics and does not promise stricter no-page/referrer measurement.
+- Release review must treat stale privacy promises as blockers when customer-visible behavior includes aggregate analytics or network services.
+
